Encapsulation and visibility.

A class that lets anyone reach in and change its fields is a class with no rules. Encapsulation is the discipline of hiding a class's internals and exposing only a controlled surface, so that the object can guarantee its own consistency. It's the first real payoff of using classes, and Java enforces it with access modifiers.

Every field and method carries a visibility, running from most open to most closed. Public means visible everywhere — that's the deliberate, published surface of the class. Protected means visible within the same package and to subclasses. If you write no modifier at all, you get package-private, visible only within the same package — that's the quiet default. And private means visible only inside this one class; nothing outside can even see it. The habit that makes encapsulation work is simple: fields private, behaviour public. State is hidden, and the only way to touch it is through methods that you control.

Why does hiding the field matter so much? Because if you leave a field public, anyone can put it into an impossible state. If balance is public, nothing stops someone assigning it minus five thousand. But make it private, and force all changes through a method, and the class can enforce its rules — what we call its invariants. In a bank account, you make balance private, and the deposit method first checks that the amount is positive, throwing an exception if it isn't, and only then adds it. You expose balance through a read-only method. Now the balance can never go negative from outside, and can never be set to a nonsense value, because there's no path to it except deposit — and a withdraw you'd write the same way. The object owns its state.

A method that reads a field is called a getter; one that writes it is a setter. They're your controlled accessors. But the point is not to mechanically wrap every single field in a get-and-set pair — that just makes fields public with extra steps. Expose only what callers genuinely need, and prefer methods that express intent: deposit of an amount, rather than set-balance; mark-shipped, rather than set-status. A blind setter for every field throws all the guarantees away again.

And here's the bigger payoff. Because callers only depend on your public methods, you're free to change how the class works on the inside — rename a field, split one into two, add a cache — without breaking a single caller. That boundary, between what I promise, the public interface, and how I do it, the private internals, is exactly what keeps large programs maintainable. Encapsulation isn't bureaucracy; it's what lets a class evolve safely.
